TI  - Polyunsaturated fatty acids in the food supply in the united states: Current trends and health implications
T2  - PROCEEDINGS OF THE WORLD CONFERENCE ON OILSEED PROCESSING AND UTILIZATION
CP  - World Conference on Oilseed Processing and Utilization
AB  - Dietary recommendations continue to evolve as we gain a better understanding of the health effects of nutrient components of the diet. The optimal levels of n-6 and n-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFA) in the diet for optimal growth and development, as well as for reduced risk of cardiovascular disease and other chronic conditions, has been the subject of significant scientific interest. In the United States, the recommended intake of n-6 PUFA (i.e., linoleic acid, LA) has been set at 1-2% of energy (en%). To date, no official recommendations have been made for n-3 PUFA in the United States, whereas other countries have adopted a variety of recommendations to increase n-3 PUFA intake. The intake of n-6 PUFA in the United States is approximately 16 g/d (6.5 en%), and for n-3 PUFA it is 1.6 g/d (0.7 en%), of which 1.2 g is alpha-linolenic acid (ALA) and 0.1-0.2 g is eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA). Recently, expert scientists have recommended an increase in n-3 PUFA intake, with ALA being set at 2.2 g/d (1 en%) and that of EPA and DHA being 0.65 g/d, and have recommended a reduction in n-6 PUFA intake to 2 en% (upper limit, 3 en%). Attaining the n-3 PUFA goals would require a fourfold increase in EPA and DHA intake and a twofold increase in ALA intake. Soybean and canola oils are the primary sources of LA and ALA in the U.S. diet, whereas EPA and DHA are derived predominantly from fish. Factors such as oil processing, cultivars with lower ALA contents, growing regions, and climatic conditions have the potential to influence n-3 PUFA dietary status. Alternative strategies such as food enrichment and biotechnology will become increasingly important in increasing intake of EPA, DHA, and ALA in the U.S. population.
