AU  - Schmitt, B
AU  - Watkinson, BM
TI  - Active substances of functional food in the prevention of arteriosclerosis -: Part 1:: Physiological effects of ω-3 fatty acids
T2  - ERNAHRUNGS UMSCHAU
AB  - Arteriosclerotic diseases have been the most frequent cause of death in Germany. Induced by chemical (hypercholesterolemia, hypertriglyceridemia. homocysteinemia) noxae, endothelial lesions and disfunctions are the initial signs of a pathological development. Influenced by immunological and oxidative mechanisms, the arteriosclerotic process advances ultimately leading to a restriction of the vascular lumen. Many epidemiological studies and clinical intervention studies suggest vasoprotective effects of long-chain omega-3 fatty acids (EPA, DHA) present mainly in food of aquatic origin. Lately the commercial supply of functional food with omega-3 fatty acids has increased. Vasoprotective effects of long-chain omega-3 fatty acid effects include a hypotriglyceridemic action, modulation of the eicosanoid and cytokine snythesis, and inhibition of blood coagulation. omega-3 fatty acids, moreover, reduce the expression of adhesion factors, stimulate endothelial vasodilatation and show slight to moderate hypotonic action. The present paper provides a survey of the pathogenesis of arteriosclerosis and the influence of omega-3 fatty acids in primary and secondary prophylaxis.
