TI  - Brain-heart connection and the risk of cardiovascular disease
T2  - CARDIOVASCULAR DIESEASES 2002
CP  - 2nd International Congress on Cardiovascular Diseases
AB  - There is evidence that interaction of stress, type A behaviour, depression with environmental factors can predispose autonomic dysfunctions leading to cardiovascular disease(CVD). Autonomic functions such as increased sympathetic and parasympathetic activity and the brains suprachiasmatic nucleus, higher nervous centres, depression, hostility, aggression appear to be important determinant of heart rate variability(HRV) which is an important risk factor of myocardial infarction, arrhythmias, sudden death, heart failure and atherosclerosis. The circadian rhythm of these complications causing increased occurrence in second quarter of the day may be due to autonomic dysfunction as well as due to presence of excitatatory brain and heart tissues. While increased sympathetic activity is associated with increased levels of cortisol, catecholamines, serotonin, renin, aldosterone, angiotensin and free radicals; increased parasympathetic activity may be associated with greater levels of acetylecholine, dopamine, nitric oxide, endorphins, coenzyme Q10,antioxidants and other protective factors. Recent studies indicate that hyperglycemia, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, ambient pollution, insulin resistance, mental stress can increase the risk of low HRV: It seems that these risk factors which are known to predispose cardiovascular disease act by decreasing HRV. There is evidence that regular fasting may modulate HRV and other risk factors of heart attack. While exercise is known to decrease HRV, exercise training may not have any adverse effect on HRV.
   Dietary n-3 fatty acids and coenzyme Q10 have been found to prevent the increased circadian occurrence of cardiac events in two separate controlled trials, possibly by increasing HRV. We have also found that n-3 fatty acids plus CoQ can decrease TNF-alpha and IL-6 in AMI which are proinflammatory agents. There is evidence that dietary n-3 fatty acids can enhance hippocampal acetylecholine levels which may be protective. Similarly, stimulation of vagus nerve may inhibit TNF synthesis in the liver and that acetylecholine, the principal vagal neurotransmitter, significantly attenuates the release of proinflammatory cytokines TNF-alpha, interleukin 1,6 and 18 but not the antiinflammatory cytokine IL-10 in experiments. Therefore any agent which can enhance brains acetylecholine may be used as therapeutic agent for increasing HRV.
