AU  - Panezai, J
AU  - Van Dyke, TE
TI  - Resolution of inflammation: Intervention strategies and future applications
T2  - TOXICOLOGY AND APPLIED PHARMACOLOGY
AB  - Chronic inflammatory diseases are the major cause of morbidity and mortality in the aging population worldwide. Chronic inflammation reflects a deficiency in the resolution phase of the acute inflammatory response, which then fails to engage the adaptive immune system accordingly. Resolution of inflammation is a tightly regulated biological pathway that sequentially aids in eliminating the inducing agent and orchestrates clearance of effete immune cells to promote the return to tissue homeostasis. The lipid mediators of resolution of inflammation comprise a family of specialized pro-resolving mediators (SPMs). The synthesis of SPMs occurs via enzymatic conversion of essential omega-6 (n-6) and omega-3 (n-3) fatty acids. SPMs have anti-inflammatory, pro-resolving and tissue regenerating properties. A large number of in vitro and in vivo studies have unveiled the mechanism of action of many SPMs. Here, we focus on the actions of SPMs in health and chronic disease models as well as their potential as therapeutic agents in ongoing and future clinical trials.
