TI  - THE TYPE OF DIETARY-FAT AFFECTS THE SEVERITY OF MYOCARDIAL LESIONS IN NZW X BXSB MICE
T2  - CORONARY ARTERY DISEASE
AB  - Whether dietary fat can affect the frequency of myocardial lesions and glomerulonephritis was determined by examining the hearts and kidneys of mice fed diets containing various fats that had subsequently become ill or died.  Death, as well as the frequency of lesions, was strikingly slowed in mice fed a diet containing quantities of omega-3 fatty acids or fish oil.  About half of those mice fed a saturated fat diet (lard) exhibited necrosis and fibrosis in sections taken from the heart, and 36% exhibited some aortic abnormalities.  Of those mice fed a corn oil diet, myocardial necrosis or fibrosis was present in all of the animals and 42% exhibited arterial abnormalities, whereas only 27% of the fish oil-fed mice had necrosis or fibrosis and 18% exhibited aortic abnormalities (P < 0.004).  Severe glomerulonephritis was a common histopathologic finding in the corn oil-fed group and least severe in the fish oil-fed group (P = 0.01).  Dietary omega-3 fatty acids appear to diminish the numbers of myocardial lesions in a strain of mice that exhibit a lupuslike syndrome and die of acute myocardial infarction.
